galatia_431	Galatia	Galatia	Galatia	Galatia	\N	Region	\N	37.5	32.5	Theographic	37.57813498250009	32.45318282875567	Related-Surrounding	Verified	\N	\N	\N	New Testament	6	1 Corinthians 16:1	Has been called the “Gallia” of the East, Roman writers calling its inhabitants Galli. They were an intermixture of Gauls and Greeks, and hence were called Gallo-Graeci, and the country Gallo-Graecia. The Galatians were in their origin a part of that great Celtic migration which invaded Macedonia about B.C. 280. They were invited by the king of Bithynia to cross over into Asia Minor to assist him in his wars.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
galeed_432	Galeed	Galeed	Galeed	Galeed	\N	Unknown	\N	32.56526757	36.00555931	Theographic	32.56526757	36.00555931	Unknown	Unverified	\N	\N	\N	Old Testament	2	Genesis 31:47	Heap of witness, the name of the pile of stones erected by Jacob and Laban to mark the league of friendship into which they entered with each other (Gen. 31:47, 48). This was the name given to the “heap” by Jacob. It is Hebrew, while the name Jegar-sahadutha, given to it by Laban, is Aramaic (Chaldee or Syriac). Probably Nahor’s family originally spoke Aramaic, and Abraham and his descendants learned Hebrew, a kindred dialect, in the land of Canaan.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
galilee_433	Galilee	Galilee	Galilee	Galilee	\N	Region	\N	32.70674542474383	35.30152807767973	Theographic	32.70674542474383	35.30152807767973	Related-Surrounding	Not identifiable	\N	\N	region	Both	63	1 Chronicles 6:76	Circuit. Solomon rewarded Hiram for certain services rendered him by the gift of an upland plain among the mountains of Naphtali. Hiram was dissatisfied with the gift, and called it “the land of Cabul” (q.v.). The Jews called it Galil.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
