sitnah_1102	Sitnah	Sitnah	Sitnah	Sitnah	\N	Unknown	\N	31.39129109635703	34.56057015639144	Theographic	31.39129109635703	34.56057015639144	Rough	Unverified	\N	\N	\N	Old Testament	1	Genesis 26:21	Strife, the second of the two wells dug by Isaac, whose servants here contended with the Philistines (Gen. 26:21). It has been identified with the modern Shutneh, in the valley of Gerar, to the west of Rehoboth, about 20 miles south of Beersheba.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
skull_1154	Skull	Skull	Skull	The Skull	\N	Unknown	\N	31.777444	35.234935	Theographic	31.777444	35.234935	Rough	Unverified	\N	\N	\N	New Testament	1	Luke 23:33	Only in Luke 23:33, the Latin name Calvaria, which was used as a translation of the Greek word Kranion, by which the Hebrew word Gulgoleth was interpreted, “the place of a skull.” It probably took this name from its shape, being a hillock or low, rounded, bare elevation somewhat in the form of a human skull. It is nowhere in Scripture called a “hill.” The crucifixion of our Lord took place outside the city walls (Heb. 13:11-13) and near the public thoroughfare.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
smyrna_1103	Smyrna	Smyrna	Smyrna	Smyrna	\N	City	\N	38.45196038	27.16192105	Theographic	38.45196038	27.16192105	Unknown	Unverified	\N	\N	\N	New Testament	2	Revelation 1:11	Myrrh, an ancient city of Ionia, on the western coast of Asia Minor, about 40 miles to the north of Ephesus. It is now the chief city of Anatolia, having a mixed population of about 200,000, of whom about one-third are professed Christians. The church founded here was one of the seven addressed by our Lord (Rev. 2:8-11). The celebrated Polycarp, a pupil of the apostle John, was in the second century a prominent leader in the church of Smyrna.	Easton's Bible Dictionary
